I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, diffraction profile, and the supplied analysis framework. Then I’ll build a candidate ledger, screen structures under common settings, and carry the defensible phase set through a staged GSAS-II refinement to the requested deliverables.

The workspace contains the raw profile plus the generator, scorer, and baseline workflow code. I’m reading the chemical/measurement context alongside the framework’s GSAS-II and scoring conventions so the refinement and exported files match what will actually be replayed and checked.

The context is a Cu Kα Bragg–Brentano scan from WO₃ + Mn₃O₄ fired at 800 °C. I’m now enumerating all Mn–W–O, precursor, and chemically plausible intermediate entries in the case’s permitted COD copy, then I’ll use peak finding/indexing to expose any phase families that those direct candidates miss.

The permitted library has one direct MnWO₄ entry, several WO₃ and Mn-oxide polymorphs, but that does not establish phase closure. I’m extracting the observed peak families and comparing full-pattern simulations, including same-topology tungstate analogues that can be substituted to Mn while preserving multiplicities.

The dominant phase is unequivocally wolframite MnWO₄: essentially every strong line from 15.47° through 99° maps to its P2/c reflection set. A second coherent family maps closely to monoclinic WO₃ (≈23.1, 26.6, 28.6, 33.3–34.2°), while the 19.9–20.4° and ~32.5° features remain outside those two models. I’m treating those as an open inventory branch rather than absorbing them into profile broadening.

I’m moving from stick-pattern matching to a reproducible GSAS-II base project. The first controlled fit will contain only the identified wolframite phase with common background/profile settings; additions will then be trialed from copies of that same base so scale collapse and residual-family removal are directly comparable.

The one-phase scale/background fit gives Rwp ≈32.7%, confirming the correct dominant topology but substantial coherent residuals. The next stage is computationally heavier; I’m checking its convergence and parameter stability before using it as the comparison baseline.

The full WO₃ polymorph screen saturated the refinement runtime when launched all at once, so I’m checking completed branches and will rerun only unfinished candidates in smaller batches. Any interrupted branch will remain “unknown,” not “rejected,” in the ledger.

The direct WO₃ enumeration is decisive: the monoclinic P2₁/c models are the only stable positive additions, reducing Rwp from 28.75% to 21.31–21.42% at the common cell-stage settings with ~17.5–17.9 wt% WO₃. Tetragonal/hexagonal alternatives either contribute weakly or fit much worse. I’m now mapping the signed residual of the best two-phase model to isolate the remaining family before testing Mn oxides and substituted Mn–W–O prototypes.

One Mn₃O₄ trial exposed an unstable GSAS branch: the scale hit its floor after a divergent iteration, so it is “unknown,” not evidence against that phase. It also revealed that GSAS auto-saves when adding a phase; I’m correcting the trial harness to copy the frozen base first and limiting the linear scale/background screen to one iteration, then regenerating the affected base.

A stable third family is now identified as hexagonal MnO (P6₃mc, COD 4117966): its scale remains positive, its cell changes by only ~0.02%, and it lowers the common-setting Rwp to 20.83% at ~7.5 wt%. Rocksalt MnO, rutile MnO₂, and Mn₃O₄ alternatives give smaller gains. I’m checking whether the 19.9–20.4° family survives this addition; if so, the inventory is still open.

The 20° family remains essentially unchanged after adding hexagonal MnO, so phase closure is not yet reached. I’m now screening chemically charge-balanced Mn₂WO₆, Mn₃WO₆, Mn₃W₂O₉, Mn₂(WO₄)₃, and topology-diverse MnWO₄ models made by one-element substitution from the permitted oxide library. Each constructed model is checked for occupancy/contact problems before profile scoring.

The substituted shortlist now has a chemically and crystallographically economical leader: Mn₂WO₆ built from the isovalent La₂WO₆ Pmn2₁ framework. Its lines account for the previously unexplained 19.9/20.4, 31.76, 39.47, 48.51, 50.16, and 59.32° family, and its cell refinement is modest. I’m replaying this and the Y/Fe/Ba topology alternatives from identical fresh projects, both with and without hexagonal MnO, to test whether MnO retains an independent scale.

The common-setting replay is still converging; the larger substituted cells make reflection generation slower, but the branches are progressing normally. I’ll report any branch that fails or distorts rather than treating it as a rejection.

The falsifiable inventory test favors four phases: wolframite MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, analogue-derived Pmn2₁ Mn₂WO₆, and hexagonal MnO. Under identical cell-stage settings, all four retain positive scales and Rwp falls to 19.65%; removing Mn₂WO₆ raises it to 20.65%, while Fe/Y/Ba framework alternatives are worse. I’m freezing this inventory and moving through instrument profile, isotropic phase broadening, then limited coordinates, with rollback checks at each stage.

The first instrument-only branch lowered Rwp to 17.34% but drove U and X negative; that branch is physically unacceptable and is being rolled back. This indicates the fixed 1 µm/1000 ppm phase broadening was already too large. I’m restarting from the simpler project with narrow, in-range phase profiles, then fitting the common instrument terms before releasing phase-specific broadening.

The phase inventory is now provisionally closed at four crystalline phases: wolframite MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, a Pmn2₁ Mn₂WO₆ analogue-derived phase, and hexagonal MnO. I’m continuing from the physically valid profile baseline, staging size/strain and structural terms one at a time; the earlier interrupted size branch remains marked unknown and won’t be used.

The dominant MnWO₄ size trial lowered Rwp only from 17.19% to 17.07% by driving the crystallite-size parameter to GSAS-II’s 10 μm ceiling. That is a boundary-hitting nuisance term, so I’m rejecting that branch and retaining the simpler 1.5 μm baseline while testing the other phases independently.

The microstrain screen is discriminating cleanly. WO₃ gives a physical 1,106 ppm and a modest stable gain; MnWO₄ goes negative, while Mn₂WO₆ and MnO require implausible 38,300 and 22,500 ppm broadening (well outside the synthesis model’s 8,000 ppm ceiling). I’m retaining only the WO₃ strain term and rolling back the other three branches.

Cell/shift replay is stable: the four cells remain chemically sensible, Mn₂WO₆ converged after a modest analogue-to-target metric adjustment, and Rwp is 15.26%. I’m moving into limited coordinate refinement now, starting with heavy-atom sublattices phase by phase so oxygen-rich models do not gain dozens of unconstrained variables at once.

A mandatory geometry audit caught a serious setting problem in the provisional Mn₂WO₆ branch: GSAS-II regenerated the Pmn2₁ equivalents in a different setting than the constructed CIF, creating 1.36 Å Mn–O contacts. I’m pausing coordinate fine-tuning and repairing/retesting that phase model; it cannot be submitted in its current form even though its peaks improve the fit.

After fixing the setting mismatch, the physically valid shortlist changes: P2₁2₁2₁ Mn₂WO₆ derived from Y₂WO₆ is the best clean topology (valid W–O ≥1.78 Å, Mn–O ≥2.15 Å), while the apparently stronger Pbcn trial needs ~1.61 Å Mn–O contacts and is not acceptable. I’m restarting the fine refinement from the clean Y-derived four-phase project; the earlier Pmn2₁ branch is excluded from the final model.

The physically constrained coordinate stage retains only modest MnWO₄ shifts; its shortest W–O/Mn–O distances remain 1.825/2.013 Å. Coordinate moves for the minor Mn₂WO₆ and MnO phases were rejected because they drove W–O or Mn–O to ~1.35/1.07 Å. I’m now testing the remaining common instrumental and low-angle terms independently.

Texture screening found one defensible term: monoclinic WO₃ [100] March–Dollase ratio 0.930, with no strong correlation. The large apparent gains for the minor Mn₂WO₆ phase require ratios 0.60 or 1.68, outside the synthesis model’s 0.7–1.4 range, so those branches are rejected as signs that the analogue assignment remains uncertain rather than evidence for extreme texture. Next I’m checking residual peak closure and phase-removal stability.

The four-phase model is physically stable, but it still leaves a coherent peak family near 20.44°, 28.72°, 35.15°, 46.51°, and 54.49°. I’m treating that as an unresolved inventory problem and am now indexing/searching oxide prototypes against those peaks before any final refinement.

A useful diagnostic is that several “missing” maxima sit just above reflections of the current Mn₂WO₆ analogue, while others track WO₃ or MnO. That means the residual may be a wrong minor-phase topology or intensity model rather than simply one more known precursor. I’m broadening the search to binary and ternary oxide prototypes with one- and two-cation substitutions, while preserving occupancies and topology.

The broad screen has 3,018 symmetry/stoichiometry-distinct oxide candidates after redox and data-quality filters. It is now calculating substituted diffraction patterns; candidates will only advance if a positive scale survives a common weighted residual test and the scaled geometry remains plausible.

About three quarters of the prototype library has passed through the structural parser. The screen is retaining disordered/fractional-occupancy parents where valid, so it can nominate solid-solution models rather than forcing every candidate to an ordered end member.

The search did not nominate another conventional Mn oxide as the main explanation. Its strongest physically viable families are substituted Mn–W oxides: rhombohedral Mn₃WO₆, orthorhombic/monoclinic Mn₂WO₅, and several symmetry-distinct Mn₂WO₆/Mn₃W₂O₉ prototypes. I’m now enumerating every exact Mn–W–O entry for those stoichiometries and will test a topology-diverse shortlist in identical GSAS-II branches; the residual-screen ranking alone is not enough to accept any of them.

The first controlled replacement screen is decisive enough to change the working hypothesis: the original P2₁2₁2₁ Mn₂WO₆ analogue is consistently worse than several independent prototypes. The best branch is monoclinic Mn₂WO₅ (P2₁/c), with orthorhombic Mn₂WO₅, MnWO₅, and rhombohedral Mn₃/₄WO₆ as alternatives. I’m continuing only that diverse shortlist to convergence and checking whether its gain survives the same advanced profile model.

The new Mn₂WO₅ topology removes the former 20.44° residual family and remains physically valid at about 8 wt%, but strong WO₃-aligned residuals remain at 23.10° and 28.60°. I’m now testing one March–Dollase term at a time for WO₃ and the minor phase; coordinate “improvements” for Mn₂WO₅ were rejected because they created 0.35–1.32 Å contacts.

The remaining width mismatch is real, but the first anisotropic-strain branch is unstable: it couples at 99.3% to the background and introduces an SVD singularity. I’m letting the other symmetry axes finish; any branch with that behavior will be classified unknown/rejected rather than used to lower Rwp.

The core four-phase model is now structurally sound and substantially improved. I’m finishing the phase-closure checks—especially whether the small cubic MnO signal is a genuine fifth phase or merely profile mismatch—before freezing and exporting the GSAS-II project.

The closure test changed the phase inventory materially: a second Mn–W–O topology contributes about 5 wt% and drops Rwp from 13.01% to 10.77%. The best current explanation is an additional orthorhombic Mn₂WO₅-like phase, but I’m treating that as a nomination—not yet a final identification—and will compare its analogues and competing stoichiometries under identical settings.

I’m now comparing every plausible Pnnm Mn₂WO₅ analogue, including the chemically closest Mn₂AsO₅ parent, against the Al₂SiO₅-derived model. Each is being tested from the same frozen four-phase project with the same background, scale, and candidate-cell freedoms; this guards against choosing a topology from one favorable starting model.

The standardized Pnnm CIF serialization uses an axis setting GSAS-II refuses. Those branches are therefore “unknown,” not rejected. I’m regenerating the same models in their original crystallographic settings and rerunning the comparison; this does not change their topology or chemistry.

The fifth phase survives a common-profile reset (Rwp 10.86% versus 13.01% without it), so its contribution is not an artifact of the initial broadening. Free isotropic size diverged to an unphysical lower bound; free isotropic strain improved the fit but overshot the ordinary isotropic range, so I am retaining the simpler stable profile while I re-optimize cells and test whether one physically interpretable anisotropic strain term is warranted.

The five-phase residual is not uniformly unassigned: the strongest peaks at 23.10°, 28.60°, 23.54°, and 35.15° coincide with modeled WO₃/Mn–W–O/MnO reflections, indicating intensity/profile mismatch. A separate family around 25.43° and 46.51° remains insufficiently explained, so I’m running a fresh oxide-prototype search on the five-phase residual before deciding closure.

The fresh search is progressing through all 3,018 admissible oxide prototypes. The key closure distinction will be whether the 25.43°/46.51° family is jointly matched by a chemically plausible topology with a stable positive scale, or only by isolated flexible decoys.

So far the search remains a broad, setting-agnostic nomination pass; no candidate is being accepted from this ranking alone. After it finishes, I’ll import only a topology- and chemistry-diverse shortlist into the same GSAS-II project and require stable scale, reasonable cell/contacts, and improvement across a peak family.

The post–five-phase search no longer nominates another Mn₂WO₅ polymorph. Its dominant chemically plausible family is rocksalt/cation-deficient MnO, followed by rutile-like mixed Mn–W oxides. I’m testing cubic MnO first, then representative mixed-oxide rutile candidates; the retained hexagonal MnO scale must remain independently positive if both polymorphs are claimed.

A sixth-phase branch is now strongly supported: a disordered rutile-like MnWO₄ analogue contributes about 2.3 wt% and lowers Rwp from 10.50% to 9.65%. Two independent P4₂/mnm parents converge to the same cell and fit, while a fractional Mn-rich rutile and cubic MnO are much weaker. I’m checking all equivalent rutile analogues and the refined local geometry before accepting this as a real minor phase.

The symmetry-diverse comparison is decisive: P4₂/mnm gives Rwp 9.65%, versus 10.07% for C2/c, 10.13% for P321, 10.19% for P‑31m/scheelite, and 10.48% for cubic MnO. I’m retaining the rutile-like phase as the sixth component, with an explicit caution that its Mn/W-disordered average geometry is only an analogue model, then re-optimizing the complete inventory.

The remaining strongest residuals now map mainly onto known WO₃ reflections rather than a coherent new-phase family. I’m moving from inventory to structural refinement: first the two symmetry-independent W sites in γ‑WO₃, then oxygen sites only where the gain is stable and all W–O/O–O contacts remain physical.

The WO₃ W-site adjustment is physically valid and reproducible when refined in blocks. Oxygen-site gains that looked larger required W–O distances of 1.36–1.47 Å and were rejected; the rutile and Pnnm coordinate branches gave only negligible or unstable gains. I’m now testing only isotropic profile terms for the two analogue phases, where broadening was initially fixed rather than determined.

The Pnnm phase has a reproducible, physically plausible broadening solution: reinitializing near 0.05 µm converges cleanly to about 0.045 µm with no high correlation and lowers Rwp to 8.95%. Its free-strain alternative required ~9,350 ppm, so crystallite size is the simpler supported interpretation. The rutile-like phase independently supports moderate microstrain (~5,270 ppm), which I’m now adding sequentially.

With the accepted size/strain terms cleaned of arbitrary starting broadening and all cells/shift re-optimized, Rwp is 8.77%. The six phase scales remain positive. I’m doing one final staged instrument recheck (W, U, X, and axial divergence separately), since the newly modeled broad minor phases can otherwise bias global peak-width parameters.

I’m also revalidating the two retained preferred-orientation corrections against the completed six-phase model. Their ratios were estimated before the broad Pnnm and rutile-like phases were discovered, so they must still improve the final inventory independently; otherwise they will be reset to unity.

Both preferred-orientation corrections remain independently necessary. WO₃ refines to a stable MD ratio of 0.907 along [100]; the P2₁/c Mn₂WO₅ phase tries to exceed the allowed orientation range when freed, so I retain its previously validated [110] ratio of 1.391 rather than the lower-Rwp extrapolation. Next I’m testing one tied isotropic displacement parameter per phase, matching the measurement model and avoiding atom-by-atom overfitting.

The displacement-parameter branches were rejected: all but WO₃ refined outside 0.003–0.020 Å² (several became negative), and WO₃’s tiny gain was scale-correlated. Polarization and surface roughness both lowered Rwp substantially but were respectively scale-correlated (|r|≈0.81) and mutually correlated/unstable (|r|≈0.95), so neither is retained. The selected model remains the simpler 8.76% fit. I’m now performing the final phase-closure search on its residual.

The final closure scan is again evaluating all 3,018 oxide prototypes against the updated residual. At this stage the leading residual peaks coincide with existing WO₃/Mn–W–O reflections; any seventh phase will be accepted only if it adds a distinct, physically credible family with a stable nontrivial fraction.

No decision is being made from the fast ranking alone. Once complete, I’ll directly import the top chemistry-diverse candidates into the frozen six-phase project; candidates that merely sharpen already modeled peaks or collapse below a defensible fraction will be rejected.

The full pattern library pass is nearly complete. The residual amplitudes are now much smaller than those that revealed the Pnnm and rutile-like phases, so I expect any further candidate to face a stringent scale-stability and geometry test.

The final search does not nominate another tungstate topology with the strength seen earlier. Its leading chemically plausible alternatives are Mn oxide polymorphs (Mn₂O₃/MnO/MnO₂); a second rutile metric is also suggested, which is more economically tested as anisotropic strain on the retained rutile phase. I’m directly screening those closure alternatives from the same final project.

The direct cell-refining screen exposed a potentially real Mn₂O₃ contribution, but its strongest P‑3m1 branch diverged before reaching a physical solution path; by the stated discipline it is “unknown,” not accepted. I’m rolling back and repeating all Mn-oxide candidates correctly in two stages—background/scales with fixed cells first, then candidate-cell refinement only for stable positive scales.

The remaining inventory question is whether the weak hexagonal MnO contribution survives once the newly supported Mn₂O₃-like phase is included. I’m testing that nested model now, then I’ll freeze and replay the physically screened final project before exporting.

The nested test resolves the overlap: hexagonal MnO falls to about 0.5 wt% and improves Rwp by only 0.016 percentage points while sharing nearly the same metric peak family as the Mn₂O₃-like phase. I’m excluding it as below reliable identifiability and retaining the simpler six-phase inventory; the report will record that branch explicitly.

The retained Mn₂O₃-like contribution is stable at roughly 4–6 wt% and supports a plausible isotropic microstrain (~3900 ppm); its crystallite-size trial hit a boundary and was rejected. A fresh residual still has broad intensity-mismatch maxima, so I’m doing one final prototype search to distinguish a genuinely missing phase from profile/structure imperfections in the accepted phases.

The search is progressing through all 3,018 oxide-parent groups, including substituted and fractional-occupancy analogues. I’ll only promote a new candidate if it gives a positive, stable scale under the same nuisance model and passes the contact/occupancy screen.

About two-thirds of the library has been screened. The residual maxima are broad enough that several simple lattices can score spuriously, so the decisive evidence will be the subsequent full GSAS-II import/refinement—not the projection rank alone.

The complete pattern generation is nearly finished. After ranking, I’ll test only a topology- and symmetry-diverse shortlist; repeated entries of the same prototype will count as one hypothesis.

The final closure screen found one materially stronger, symmetry-distinct hypothesis: an orthorhombic Pna2₁ MnO₂ analogue contributes about 3.6 wt% even before cell refinement (Rwp 8.21 → 7.91). Four control prototypes are positive but only ~0.4–0.7 wt%. I’m now refining each candidate cell under identical settings to see whether the MnO₂ branch remains uniquely stable and physical.

The Pna2₁ phase remains a ~4.5 wt% contribution after resetting its profile to neutral values. Its size refinement is unstable and its strain term is only marginal, so both are being left fixed. I’m testing only small coordinate blocks now; any branch that creates short Mn–O/O–O contacts will be discarded even if Rwp drops.

After adding Pna2₁ MnO₂, the remaining “extra phase” fits are all below 0.5 wt% and cluster around a second rutile-like lattice. That pattern is more consistent with an imperfect profile for the already modeled rutile-type MnWO₄ than with four distinct chemistries, so I’m testing the generator-supported uniaxial microstrain alternatives before deciding whether any eighth phase is defensible.

The rutile-like addition is reproducibly positive at ~0.49 wt%; once included, the competing WO₂/I4₁md/Mn₃WO₇ branches collapse below 0.06 wt%, which supports one minor rutile-family contribution rather than several. A separate cubic Fd-3m Mn₂O₃ analogue still contributes about 1 wt% and lowers Rwp materially, so it now requires its own cell-refined test rather than being dismissed as a decoy.

The nine-phase model now closes the candidate inventory: after the cubic Mn₂O₃ phase is added, the remaining top residual maxima coincide with strong reflections of already modeled WO₃, wolframite, Mn₂WO₅, and MnO₂ and show neighboring sign changes, rather than forming a new unassigned peak family. I’m doing the final common re-optimization and will retain only profile terms that remain stable and within the laboratory’s physical ranges.

The final common refinement is stable at Rwp 7.51% (Rp 5.58%, GOF 3.77). The dominant phase fractions are now fixed; the second rutile-family contribution has fallen to 0.28 wt%, so I will report it as detected by the profile model but not reliably identifiable or quantifiable. I’m now replaying the frozen project and auditing every exported CIF for occupancy, density, symmetry, and short contacts.

The artifact checks are clean: all nine CIFs pass the scorer’s occupancy/contact screen, their hashes exactly match the structures replay-exported from `final.gpx`, and the full-grid profile is accepted. One ledger audit found two exact MnO₂ database entries that had been structurally inspected but not given explicit final-model scale trials; I’m closing those two bookkeeping branches now before writing the report.

Both omitted exact MnO₂ entries failed GSAS-II import because their published CIF symmetry settings are incompatible; per the ledger discipline they are currently “unknown,” not rejected. I’m converting each to a conventional setting without changing composition or topology, then repeating the scale tests.

The final replay and consistency checks have passed: nine active phases, normalized fractions summing to 1, all CIFs byte-identical to the project’s own replay exports, and `fit.csv` accepted on the complete measured grid. I’ve also recorded every accepted/rejected branch and the low-confidence 0.28 wt% component explicitly.

